WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:04.409
In this lesson we will see how to find the
unknowns

2
00:00:04.409 --> 00:00:07.110
by Cramer's rule and derive the formulas.

3
00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:10.348
Then we will move on to examples and describe
in detail

4
00:00:10.348 --> 00:00:13.350
how to solve a system of linear algebraic
equations

5
00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:16.310
by Cramer's rule.

6
00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:24.860
Cramer's rule is used to solve systems of
linear algebraic equations in which

7
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:27.651
the number of unknowns equals the number of
equations

8
00:00:27.651 --> 00:00:30.020
and the determinant of the coefficient matrix

9
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:36.610
is nonzero. Let a system of three linear
equations in three unknowns be given.

10
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:44.620
The coefficient matrix of such a system is
square. The determinant of this matrix

11
00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.551
is called the determinant of the system.

12
00:00:47.551 --> 00:00:51.580
If the determinant of the system is nonzero,
the system

13
00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.660
is called nonsingular.

14
00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:59.057
If Δ is not zero, we find the solution of this

15
00:00:59.057 --> 00:01:02.120
system of equations. We form auxiliary

16
00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.709
determinants: these are the determinants of
the matrices obtained

17
00:01:05.709 --> 00:01:08.580
by replacing each column of matrix A with the
column

18
00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:13.800
of constant terms.

19
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:16.930
Cramer's formulas look like this.

20
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:24.295
Let us look at an example: take a system of
three linear equations

21
00:01:24.295 --> 00:01:27.940
in three unknowns, which we will solve by
Cramer's rule.

22
00:01:28.450 --> 00:01:35.420
We form the determinant of the matrix and find
its value. Δ is not zero,

23
00:01:35.420 --> 00:01:39.460
so the system has a unique solution.

24
00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:47.920
Now we find the values of the auxiliary
determinants: first Δx₁,

25
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:50.120
then Δx₂,

26
00:01:50.990 --> 00:01:53.930
and finally Δx₃.

27
00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:02.720
Now we substitute them into the final formulas
and find the values of the variables.

28
00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.817
To sum up: Cramer's rule lets us find the
solution

29
00:02:08.817 --> 00:02:11.800
of a system of linear algebraic equations

30
00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:17.980
if the determinant of the coefficient matrix
is nonzero.

31
00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:24.220
In essence, the method relies on computing
determinants of n×n matrices

32
00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:31.080
and applying the corresponding formulas to
find the unknowns.
